A3. `WrittenNameScanner` — NEW file; mechanics EXTRACTED from `ContractConcreteTypeMustNotBeCastToAnalyzer.cs:82` (`GetSymbolInfo(typeSyntax).Symbol is INamedTypeSymbol`) and `AttributeIdentity.cs:69` (the `GetTypeInfo` tier). Owns "this node writes a name; here is the symbol it binds to and the type that name denotes". No access policy.

```csharp
internal static class WrittenNameScanner
{
    // The two concrete SimpleNameSyntax kinds. Every written name bottoms out at one of them.
    internal static readonly ImmutableArray<SyntaxKind> NameKinds =
        ImmutableArray.Create(SyntaxKind.IdentifierName, SyntaxKind.GenericName);

    internal static void Register(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol, INamedTypeSymbol> inspect)
    {
        context.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(c => Dispatch(c, inspect), NameKinds);
    }

    internal static void RegisterForAssembly(
        CompilationStartAnalysisContext context,
        string assemblyName,
        Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol, INamedTypeSymbol> inspect)
    {
        if (!OwnerClass.InAssembly(assemblyName)(context.Compilation)) return;
        Register(context, inspect);
    }

    // The judgment, pure and directly callable — this is what the direct scanner tests drive.
    internal static (ISymbol? Symbol, INamedTypeSymbol? DenotedType) Resolve(
        SemanticModel semanticModel, SimpleNameSyntax name, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        ISymbol? bound = semanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(name, cancellationToken).Symbol
            ?? RecoverBoundType(semanticModel, name, cancellationToken);
        return (bound, bound as INamedTypeSymbol ?? bound?.ContainingType);
    }

    // Tier 2: the non-error named type the node's TYPE info yields when no symbol binds. AttributeIdentity's
    // second tier is this same recovery; it calls here rather than spelling it a second time.
    internal static INamedTypeSymbol? RecoverBoundType(
        SemanticModel semanticModel, SyntaxNode node, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        return semanticModel.GetTypeInfo(node, cancellationToken).Type
            is INamedTypeSymbol { TypeKind: not TypeKind.Error } recovered ? recovered : null;
    }

    private static void Dispatch(
        SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext context, Action<SyntaxNodeAnalysisContext, ISymbol, INamedTypeSymbol> inspect)
    {
        (ISymbol? symbol, INamedTypeSymbol? denoted) = Resolve(
            context.SemanticModel, (SimpleNameSyntax)context.Node, context.CancellationToken);
        if (symbol is not null && denoted is not null) inspect(context, symbol, denoted);
    }
}
```

`RegisterForAssembly` reuses `OwnerClass.InAssembly` (`OwnerClass.cs:107`), the existing owner of the compilation-assembly-name comparison, rather than adding a third inline copy.

Why the callback hands back BOTH symbol and denoted type, measured: an attribute's own name node binds through `GetSymbolInfo` to the attribute CONSTRUCTOR, not the type (`[Guarded(1)]` → `Method:N.GuardedAttribute.GuardedAttribute(int)`). A consumer testing only `symbol is INamedTypeSymbol` silently misses every guarded type used as an attribute — which the contract's MUST NOT calls a defect, not a boundary. The `symbol as INamedTypeSymbol ?? symbol.ContainingType` collapse is the same collapse `AttributeIdentity.ResolveAttributeType` performs in its tier 1, so it is extraction, not invention. It loses nothing: for `Array.Empty<GuardedType>()` the denoted type is `System.Array`, but the type-argument leaf `GuardedType` is visited as its own `IdentifierName` node.

====================================================================
B. `CompositionPoint`'s method-level check — and a MEASURED DEFECT in the obvious version
====================================================================

```csharp
// Added to CompositionPoint.cs beside the existing type-level sites.
internal static bool IsContainerFactory(ISymbol? symbol)
{
    return symbol is IMethodSymbol { IsStatic: true } method
        && string.Equals(method.Name, ContainerFactoryMethodName, StringComparison.Ordinal)
        && WellKnownType.IsInAssembly(
            method.ContainingType, EngineAssembly.Name, ContainerFactoryTypeName, EngineAssembly.Name);
}

internal static bool IsInsideContainerFactory(ISymbol containingSymbol, SyntaxNode site)
{
    return IsContainerFactory(containingSymbol) && IsInOwnBody(site);
}

private static bool IsInOwnBody(SyntaxNode site)
{
    for (SyntaxNode? current = site; current is not null; current = current.Parent)
    {
        switch (current)
        {
            case AnonymousFunctionExpressionSyntax:
            case LocalFunctionStatementSyntax:
                return false;
            case BaseMethodDeclarationSyntax:
            case AccessorDeclarationSyntax:
            case TypeDeclarationSyntax:
                return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

THE DEFECT, measured through the real analyzer pipeline on an `AgentGuard.Engine` compilation: `context.ContainingSymbol` reports `AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices.Create()` for a call written inside a lambda AND inside a local function nested in `Create()`'s body — `methodKind=Ordinary` in all three cases. A caller check built on `ContainingSymbol` alone therefore PERMITS exactly what contract Acceptance 12 requires all three rules to REPORT. `CompositionPoint.EnclosedBy` (the ledger's named extraction source, `CompositionPoint.cs:94`) walks enclosing TYPES and is more permissive still. The `IsInOwnBody` syntax conjunction is not decoration; without it Acceptance 12 cannot pass. Measured split with the conjunction in place:

| site | ContainingSymbol | IsInOwnBody | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| `DoorType.Create()` direct in `Create()` | `SystemServices.Create()` | True | permitted ✓ |
| same call inside a lambda in `Create()` | `SystemServices.Create()` | **False** | reported ✓ Acc. 12 |
| same call inside a local function in `Create()` | `SystemServices.Create()` | **False** | reported ✓ Acc. 12 |
| same call in `SystemServices.Elsewhere()` | `SystemServices.Elsewhere()` | True | reported ✓ |

Both lenses supply the site node: `context.Operation.Syntax` for `MemberUseScanner`, `context.Node` for `WrittenNameScanner`, and `DeclaredTypeUse.Location` plus the declarator syntax for `DeclaredTypeScanner`.

AG0041 uses `IsContainerFactory` (the method IDENTITY, as a permitted CALLEE), not `IsInsideContainerFactory` — it restricts what is reached, never who reaches it; AG0017 already pins the callers. Its exemption is two nodes, measured: `SystemServices.Create()` fires the written-name lens twice, once on the receiver (`NamedType`) and once on `Create` (`Method`), both with denoted type `SystemServices`. Exempt (a) any symbol satisfying `IsContainerFactory`, and (b) the `SystemServices` type node whose enclosing member-access binds to that same identity. Under `using static AgentGuard.Engine.SystemServices;` the bare `Create` binds to the identical method symbol (measured), so (a) covers it, while the directive's own `SystemServices` leaf has a `QualifiedName` parent and no bound `Create` sibling, so it is reported — matching the brief's established fact.

====================================================================
C. RULING ON THE `AttributeIdentity` TWO-TIER COMPARISON (live Roslyn 4.14.0)
====================================================================

Measured on name nodes, not on `AttributeSyntax`:

| fixture | `GetSymbolInfo().Symbol` | `CandidateReason` / count | `GetTypeInfo().Type` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `[Guarded(1)]`, ctor binds | `Method:N.GuardedAttribute.GuardedAttribute(int)` | None / 0 | `NamedType:N.GuardedAttribute` |
| `[Guarded]`, required arg omitted | **null** | OverloadResolutionFailure / 1 | **`NamedType:N.GuardedAttribute`** |
| `[N.Guarded]`, arg omitted | null | OverloadResolutionFailure / 1 | `NamedType:N.GuardedAttribute` |
| `[GuardedAttribute]`, arg omitted | null | OverloadResolutionFailure / 1 | `NamedType:N.GuardedAttribute` |
| `[Guarded<int>]`, arg omitted | null | OverloadResolutionFailure / 1 | `NamedType:N.GuardedAttribute<int>` |

RULING, three parts.

C1. The second tier is REQUIRED for `WrittenNameScanner`, not optional. `AttributeIdentity`'s documented case — "a required constructor argument omitted … fails overload resolution and yields no symbol even though the attribute's TYPE still binds" — reproduces exactly at the name-node level, for the plain, qualified, full-`…Attribute`-spelling and generic-attribute forms. Single-tier resolution silently drops every guarded type used as a malformed attribute, in all four spellings. Confirmed end-to-end through `CompilationWithAnalyzers`: the two-tier stand-in reports `tier2(GetTypeInfo) symbol=NamedType:AgentGuard.Engine.GuardedAttribute`.

C2. The shared mechanic is TIER 2 ONLY. Tier 1 differs in output shape by design — `AttributeIdentity` narrows a constructor to its containing type; `WrittenNameScanner` must keep the symbol, because AG0041 governs internal Engine MEMBERS as well as types ("an internal Engine member named anywhere other than the approved call"). Forcing one tier-1 body on both would break AG0041. `WrittenNameScanner.Resolve` reuses the tier-1 *collapse* for its denoted-type output instead, which is where the two genuinely agree.

C3. `AttributeIdentity.ResolveAttributeType` collapses to one line and keeps identical behaviour:
```csharp
if (semanticModel.GetSymbolInfo(attribute, cancellationToken).Symbol is IMethodSymbol constructor
    && constructor.ContainingType is { TypeKind: not TypeKind.Error } fromConstructor) return fromConstructor;
return WrittenNameScanner.RecoverBoundType(semanticModel, attribute, cancellationToken);
```
`AttributeIdentity.cs` is NOT in this contract's Surfaces, so this is D2 for Tim. The third tier — the syntactic `…Attribute` fallback for a genuinely undefined identifier — stays with AG0008/AG0105, as the brief directs, and `WrittenNameScanner` does NOT get one: an unresolved name in an Engine/CLI/TestHelpers compilation cannot be judged against an assembly-and-namespace identity, and inventing a name match there would make the guarded-type set a bare-name match. Fail-closed here is the compiler's job — `AnalyzerRunner` already throws unless every fixture produces exactly the compiler errors it declares.

